<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the best snow boots are waterproof, insulated for the temperatures you'll face, and have deep, grippy soles for ice and packed snow. Choose a height that keeps snow out, leave room for a warm sock, and pick a style for how you'll use them: lighter, more flexible boots for town and travel, and taller, more insulated boots for deep snow and cold days.</p></div>

<h2>What snow boots are for</h2>
<p>Snow boots aren't ski or snowboard boots. They're what you wear around the base area, in town, on winter walks, and on the drive to the mountain. They're also a must for snowshoeing (with snowshoes strapped on) and for anyone who spends time outside in winter.</p>

<h2>What to look for</h2>
<h3>Waterproofing</h3>
<p>Look for a waterproof membrane or fully waterproof lower (rubber or sealed leather), plus a gusseted tongue so snow and slush can't get in through the laces. "Water-resistant" boots are fine for dry cold but soak through in slush.</p>
<h3>Insulation</h3>
<p>Insulation is often listed in grams or with a temperature rating. More insulation means warmer but bulkier boots. Temperature ratings are a rough guide, not a guarantee: how warm a boot feels depends on your socks, activity, and circulation. If you stand still a lot, choose more insulation; if you'll be walking or hiking, choose less so your feet don't sweat.</p>
<h3>Traction</h3>
<p>Deep lugs and soft rubber grip snow and ice better than shallow, hard soles. For icy sidewalks or trails, add traction devices that slip over your boots.</p>
<h3>Height</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Ankle height:</strong> lighter and easier to drive in; fine for cleared streets.</li>
  <li><strong>Mid-calf:</strong> the most versatile height for snowy towns and base areas.</li>
  <li><strong>Tall:</strong> best for deep snow and unplowed paths.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Fit</h3>
<p>Try boots on with the socks you'll wear. Leave a little room to wiggle your toes: tight boots restrict circulation and make your feet colder. Removable liners are a plus because you can dry them overnight.</p>

<h2>Snow boots for women</h2>
<p>Women's snow boots are built on a narrower last, with a lower-volume heel and a different calf shape. If women's boots feel tight at the calf, look for adjustable or wide-calf styles. Many women's boots lean toward fashion; check the waterproofing, insulation, and sole before choosing one for real snow. If you have wider feet, men's or unisex sizes can work well; just adjust for size differences.</p>

<h2>Snow boots by use</h2>
