<h2>Snow boots vs winter hiking boots</h2>
<p>Snow boots prioritize warmth and keeping snow out, with thick insulation, tall shafts, and roomy fits. Winter hiking boots are lighter, more supportive, and better for long walks on packed trails, but usually have less insulation. If you mostly stand around the base area, cheer at races, or walk around town, snow boots are warmer. If you plan to hike or snowshoe for hours, a supportive insulated hiking boot may be more comfortable.</p>

<h2>Care and storage</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Knock off snow and pull liners out to dry after each day.</li>
  <li>Dry boots at room temperature, away from direct heat that can crack leather and rubber.</li>
  <li>Clean off road salt, which can stain and break down materials.</li>
  <li>Reapply waterproofing treatment to leather or fabric uppers as the maker recommends.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Keep your feet warm</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Wear one pair of wool or synthetic socks, not cotton.</li>
  <li>Don't lace boots so tight they cut off circulation.</li>
  <li>Dry boots and liners overnight; damp boots are cold boots.</li>
  <li>Add toe warmers on very cold days.</li>
</ul>
